1 · Track: secure the grid - The Problem: “Today’s locks expire in 2030”
Certain current encryption methods will be rendered obselete by quantum computing
Attackers record encrypted utility traffic now, to unlock it once quantum computers arrive.
NIST deadline: today’s encryption is phased out by 2030 and banned by 2035.
Utilities can’t afford premium security on every cable, and nothing tells them where it matters most.

2 · Customers: “One tool, every critical network”

First customer: utilities like EPB. Users are security engineers; the decision maker is the security chief facing NERC CIP rules.
Why they’d buy: a fixed deadline, a fixed budget, and a plan they can defend to regulators.
Sector table:
Power: load in MW
Water: people served
Pipeline: barrels per day
Hospital: beds
Bank: dollars per day
For each one, the table says what’s at risk and how the plan changes.
Bottom line: same deadline, same buyer, same product.
Live moment: switch the sector in the tool.

3 · Solution: “QKD Grid Optimizer”

Load a map, draw one, or import a photo of one, and get a plan in under a second.
Quantum-safe software on every link; quantum “intruder alarm” hardware only where it pays off; backup routes.
Chattanooga results: about 1 in 8 links gets hardware, covering 23% of the load and cutting the worst wiretap by 48%.
Tested on 21 networks; ran on IonQ and Quantinuum backends through qBraid.
Honest limits: hardware price and field trials are unproven, and quantum computers didn’t beat classical ones at optimization & planning.

