Quantum algorithms simulated on a laptop (classical)
QAOA on 19 networks (6–14 qubits). Exact numpy state-vector simulation, comparing standard and XY-mixer QAOA against random guessing.
BB84 alarm studies (3a, 3d). Thousands of BB84 batches on Qiskit Aer with 2.5% line noise, used to set the alarm level and measure the smallest tap it catches.
Purely classical
Grid extraction: the 21 networks pulled from the Texas A&M synthetic grid file, with line lengths estimated from line reactance.
Optimizer: an exact integer program (SciPy’s HiGHS solver), which picks the QKD links. The web page uses a fast heuristic, checked against the exact solver.
Benchmark: 8 algorithms on 192 test cases.
Sobol sensitivity analysis: 11 parameters across 3 cities.
SMAA: 400 re-solves with uncertain parameter values.
Attack simulations: wiretaps, random taps, relay compromise, detector blinding, jamming and cable cuts, across 21 networks and 3 budgets.
Resilience: backup routes (graph bridge removal) and key splitting.
Alarm design: Kalman filter plus CUSUM, and fusion with fiber monitoring.
Key-rate estimate: a loss model fitted to EPB’s measured links.
Image to grid: qBraid AI Gateway, which is a classical AI model even though it’s a qBraid feature.

How to say it in one line: “The quantum part is the key exchange and its eavesdropper alarm, which we ran on IonQ hardware and three qBraid simulators and emulators. The planning is classical because, when we tested QAOA, the classical solver won.”

Two numbers to keep straight in the pitch:

The 0.004 baseline is the only real-hardware number unless the Forte attack run finishes.
The 24–26% tap figures come from simulators and an emulator, so say “measured on IonQ and Quantinuum backends,” not “on real quantum hardware.”
